System for classifying segmented text lines as printed or handwritten.

Install with Python 3.9 or 3.10:

    python -m pip install -e .

For the OCR-D command, install the optional dependencies:

    python -m pip install -e '.[ocrd]'

The supported PyTorch pair is Torch 1.13 and Torchvision 0.14. Runtime
dependency ranges are maintained in requirements.txt and read by
pyproject.toml. OCR-D dependencies are in requirements-ocrd.txt.
The internal Python package retains its original module name so existing
serialized model files load without conversion.
The provided neural network has been trained on:
* a private dataset for handwritten text lines
  + 9577 training lines
  + 1562 validation lines
* a dataset of printed text which will be released before August 2019
  + its address will be added here - if not, please send a reminder
  + 40459 training lines
  + 1562 test lines
The accuracy of the classifier on the validation data is 97.47%.

If you intend to use it, it is highly advised that you train it on your
own data. To do so, create the following folders:
  lines/training/handwritten
  lines/training/printed
  lines/validation/handwritten
  lines/validation/printed
and place your line images in these folders. Train the model with
`printed-vs-handwritten-train`, and evaluate it with `printed-vs-handwritten-evaluate`. Training
writes models/classifier.tgc in the working directory. Evaluation uses that
file when present, otherwise the bundled model.

Usage example:
printed-vs-handwritten 616.jpg

To extract line images from PAGE-XML in the TESS file group of every
`ppns/PPN*` workspace, install `ocrd` and `ocrd-segment-extract-lines`, then run:

    make extract

The target registers images from `DEFAULT/` with the OCR-D Python API when
that file group is absent from the workspace METS file. Image page IDs are
`PHYS_0000` and METS file IDs are `FILE_0000_DEFAULT`, with `0000` taken
from the last four digits of each image filename.
Output goes to `TESS-LINES`;
override it with `make extract EXTRACT_GRP=OTHER-GROUP` or use a different
workspace root with `make extract PPNS=/path/to/ppns`.
